  "feeling": "Sorrow, a misread kindness",
  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Fog",
      "science": "When signals are noisy, a kind act can look like a slight. Then two fair creatures can drift into a feud that neither one chose.",
      "limits": "The fog flips what one side sees. It never changes what happened.",
      "counter": "Some say intent shows through in the end, fog or no fog.",
      "question": "How much forgiveness does a foggy world need?",
      "sources": [
        {
          "label": "The Evolution of Trust, Nicky Case",
          "url": "https://ncase.me/trust/words.html"
        },
        {
          "label": "How to Cope with Noise in the Iterated Prisoner's Dilemma, Wu and Axelrod, 1995",
          "url": "https://ideas.repec.org/a/sae/jocore/v39y1995i1p183-189.html"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Echo's slip",
      "science": "A strict copycat repeats your last move. After one misread gift it hits back, and then two copycats trade blows forever. A copycat that sometimes forgives breaks the loop.",
      "limits": "Here forgiveness is one dial on each creature.",
      "counter": "A forgiving copycat can be used by creatures who never share.",
      "question": "What breaks a revenge loop?",
      "sources": [
        {
          "label": "A Strategy of Win-Stay, Lose-Shift, Nowak and Sigmund, 1993",
          "url": "https://doi.org/10.1038/364056a0"
        },
        {
          "label": "Slow to Anger and Fast to Forgive, Fudenberg, Rand and Dreber, 2012",
          "url": "https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.102.2.720"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "diary": {
    "echo": [
      "I do to you what you did to me last time. It seemed fair. In the fog I cannot tell what you did."
    ]
  }
